\section{The constraints are more restrictive than they look}{


\code{max_peptide_length} is 30 for most models, which excludes a substantial fraction of real
tryptic peptides - and every one of them comes back as \code{NA} with a warning rather than an error.

\code{allowed_unimod_ids} being \strong{empty means the model accepts no modifications at all}, which is a
real answer rather than a missing one.

}
\section{Constraints are a tri-state, not a list}{


\code{precursor_charge}, \code{collision_energy}, \code{instrument_type} and \code{fragmentation_type} each hold an
\code{mzlibr_constraint} whose \code{requirement} is one of \code{"not_applicable"} (the model has no such
input - do not send it), \code{"any_value_required"} (you must send one, any value) or \code{"one_of"}
(you must send one of its \code{values}).

mzLib encodes all three states in one nullable set, where \code{NULL} means *not applicable* and an
*empty* set means *required, any value*. Read straight, that makes \code{Prosit_2020_intensity_CID}
look permissive about collision energy - it accepts none, being fixed at NCE 35 - and
\code{Prosit_2020_intensity_HCD} look like it accepts none, when it requires one.

}
